LOESS reference implementation
------------------------------
packages/core/src/stats/loess.ts adapts its structure (the sorted
nearest-neighbor window walk and the tricube kernel) from SveltePlot's
`src/regression/loess.ts` (ISC License, Copyright (c) Gregor Aisch and
SveltePlot contributors), which is itself adapted from:

  - d3-regression, Copyright (c) Harry Stevens (MIT License)
    https://github.com/HarryStevens/d3-regression
  - science.js, Copyright (c) Jason Davies (BSD-3-Clause License)
    https://github.com/jasondavies/science.js
  - vega-statistics, Copyright (c) University of Washington Interactive
    Data Lab (BSD-3-Clause License)
    https://github.com/vega/vega

The ggsvelte implementation is substantially rewritten for parity with R's
stats::loess (span-based neighborhoods, configurable polynomial degree,
prediction at arbitrary points, and exact operator statistics for the
confidence band); no verbatim code from the above projects remains, but the
algorithmic lineage is acknowledged here per their license terms and the
project plan.

Kyoto cherry-blossom full-bloom dates (bundled teaching dataset)
----------------------------------------------------------------
packages/svelte/src/lib/data/kyoto-sakura.ts and the docs asset
apps/docs/static/kyoto-sakura.json contain peak-bloom dates for Kyoto,
Japan, 812–2026 CE.

  Data copyright holder: Yasuyuki Aono (Osaka Prefecture University).
  Original source: http://atmenv.envi.osakafu-u.ac.jp/aono/kyophenotemp4/
  (closed 2025-03-31; Aono's file KyotoFullFlower7.xls, covering 801–2015,
  is archived at https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/pub/data/paleo/historical/
  phenology/japan/LatestVersion/)
  - 9th–14th centuries acquired and analyzed by Aono and Saito (2010),
    International Journal of Biometeorology 54, 211–219.
  - 15th–21st centuries acquired and analyzed by Aono and Kazui (2008),
    International Journal of Climatology 28, 905–914.
  - Modern observations are made by a local newspaper in Arashiyama, Kyoto.
  - The series is continued after Aono's death by Genki Katata (Canon
    Institute for Global Studies), https://cigs.canon/katata_date/, which
    is the source for the years following Aono's own file.

The series was retrieved via the cleaned mirror published for the George
Mason University peak-bloom-prediction competition
(https://github.com/GMU-CherryBlossomCompetition/peak-bloom-prediction,
data/kyoto.csv), whose repository content is CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, with the most
recent years checked against Katata's table; the Kyoto observations
themselves carry Aono's copyright notice above. ggsvelte
redistributes the observation dates — factual measurements, reproduced
without the mirror's editorial content — with the attribution required by
the source. Anyone publishing charts made from this data should cite Aono
as above (see the KYOTO_SAKURA_CITATION export).

Binning, boxplot, density, and summary algorithms are independent ports of
the published ggplot2 (MIT) and R (GPL — algorithms only, no code copied)
semantics, verified against R-generated fixtures; see
docs/decisions/0010-m2-stats-notes.md.

Example gallery datasets
------------------------
Several datasets under examples/*/*/data.ts are transcribed from HistData
(https://github.com/friendly/HistData), an R package of historical datasets
in the history of statistics and data visualization, maintained by Michael
Friendly. The HistData package is distributed under the GPL; each dataset's
`data.ts` header names the dataset and its original source.

The underlying tables are digitizations of works published before 1930 —
among them Halley (1693), Playfair (1821, 1824), Quetelet (1846), Farr
(1852), Snow (1855), Nightingale (1858), Minard (1869), Guerry (1833),
Darwin (1876), Michelson (1879), Galton (1886) and Herschel (1833) — whose
originals are long out of copyright. ggsvelte makes no public-domain claim
about the HistData package itself; this notice credits HistData and its
maintainer as the proximate source of the transcriptions.

Individual datasets carrying additional provenance, credited where used:
  - Snow.* — modern digitization of Snow's Broad Street map by Rusty Dodson
    and Waldo Tobler
  - Guerry — digitization by Stephane Dray and Michael Friendly
  - Langren — reconstruction by Michael Friendly
  - Cholera — transcription by Nicholas Verlander

Three teaching tables under examples/*/*/data.ts are transcribed instead from
the `datasets` package that ships with R (https://www.r-project.org/), which
is distributed under the GPL. As with HistData, the credit owed is to the
people who made the measurements:

  - `faithful` (examples/bin2d/basic) — eruption and waiting times for the
    Old Faithful geyser, sourced by R from W. Härdle, "Smoothing Techniques
    with Implementation in S" (Springer, 1991). Azzalini and Bowman, Applied
    Statistics 39 (1990), 357–365, published a fuller version of the record.
  - `quakes` (examples/hex/basic) — 1,000 seismic events near Fiji, one of
    the Harvard PRIM-H project data sets, obtained by that project from
    Dr John Woodhouse of the Harvard Department of Geophysics.
  - `volcano` (examples/contour/basic) — topography of Maunga Whau,
    Auckland, digitized from a topographic map by Ross Ihaka. R's own note
    that these heights should not be regarded as accurate is repeated in the
    example's data.ts header.

The Palmer Archipelago penguin measurements under examples/interaction/*/data.ts
come from the palmerpenguins R package (https://allisonhorst.github.io/palmerpenguins/)
by Allison Horst, Alison Hill and Kristen Gorman, released under CC0 1.0
(public domain dedication). The underlying data were collected by Dr Kristen
Gorman at Palmer Station, Antarctica, as part of the Palmer Long Term
Ecological Research programme, and are published by the Environmental Data
Initiative. The package authors ask that the LTER programme be acknowledged
in any publication using the data, which this notice does.

Palmer Archipelago penguins (bundled teaching dataset)
------------------------------------------------------
packages/svelte/src/lib/data/palmer-penguins.ts and the docs asset
apps/docs/static/palmer-penguins.json contain 333 complete penguin body
measurements (Adelie, Chinstrap, Gentoo) from Palmer Station, Antarctica,
2007–2009.

  Collected by Dr Kristen Gorman at Palmer Station as part of the Palmer
  Long Term Ecological Research programme; published by the Environmental
  Data Initiative.
  Package authors: Allison Horst, Alison Hill and Kristen Gorman.
  Source package: https://allisonhorst.github.io/palmerpenguins/
  License: CC0 1.0 Universal (public domain dedication).

ggsvelte redistributes the complete-cases rows (11 incomplete of 344 dropped)
with the LTER acknowledgement the package authors request. Anyone publishing
charts made from this data should cite the package authors and acknowledge
Palmer LTER (see the PALMER_PENGUINS_CITATION export).

EPA fuel economy — popular models (bundled teaching dataset)
------------------------------------------------------------
packages/svelte/src/lib/data/mpg.ts and the docs asset apps/docs/static/mpg.json
contain 234 vehicle configurations for 38 popular models in model years 1999
and 2008.

  Underlying measurements: U.S. Environmental Protection Agency fuel-economy
  data published at https://fueleconomy.gov/. U.S. government works are not
  subject to copyright in the United States.
  Popular-model subset and column layout: ggplot2::mpg
  (https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/mpg.html), MIT License.

ggsvelte redistributes the same popular-model subset used as ggplot2's classic
teaching table. Cite the EPA source for the measurements and ggplot2 for the
subset definition when publishing charts (see the MPG_CITATION export).

